@startuml paradigm-dominance
' ParadigmProfile::dominant_paradigm — the two-test decision rule that turns
' four scores into one verdict (or None, or Mixed). The thresholds are literals
' inside the method, NOT configuration fields.
' Colour encodes outcome: abstain (slate), Mixed (light purple), decided (green).
' Render: plantuml -tsvg docs/diagrams/paradigm-dominance.puml
' Source of truth for Figure 2 in docs/components/paradigm/indicators.md §5
skinparam backgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center
skinparam ArrowColor #37474F
skinparam ArrowFontColor #37474F
skinparam activity {
  BackgroundColor #6A1B9A
  BorderColor #263238
  FontColor white
  FontStyle bold
  DiamondBackgroundColor #FFF3E0
  DiamondBorderColor #EF6C00
  DiamondFontColor #263238
  StartColor #455A64
  EndColor #455A64
}
title ParadigmProfile::dominant_paradigm() → Option<Paradigm>

start
:sort the four scores descending;
:S₍₁₎ ← highest score
S₍₂₎ ← runner-up;
if (S₍₁₎ < θ = 0.20 ?) then (yes — nothing\nclears the floor)
  :**None**
  the sample is too thin to call; <<#455A64>>
  stop
else (no)
endif
if (S₍₁₎ − S₍₂₎ < δ = 0.10 ?) then (yes — the top\ntwo are too close)
  :**Some(Mixed)**
  genuinely multi-paradigm
  (e.g. Rust: impl + iter.map); <<#AB47BC>>
  stop
else (no — a clear lead)
endif
:**Some(top paradigm)**
one style dominates; <<#2E7D32>>
stop

note right
  θ = 0.20 and δ = 0.10 are **literals**
  in the method body. They are **not**
  configurable — ParadigmConfig carries
  no dominance field. To impose your own
  thresholds, call present_paradigms(θ)
  and apply your own margin test to the
  returned, descending-sorted list.
end note
@enduml
